#   Shell scripts MUST check out with LF endings, on every platform.
#
#   This repo is worked on with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites text files to CRLF in the
# working tree. For C sources that is harmless. For a shell script it is not: bash reads the
# trailing \r as part of each command and fails with `$'\r': command not found`, or worse,
# silently passes an argument with a carriage return glued to the end of it. The script is not
# broken and does not look broken -- it is a checkout artifact, and it only appears on a fresh
# clone, which is the least convenient moment to meet it.
#
#   this repo currently contains no shell scripts -- scripts/light-coverage.sh became
# light-coverage-worker.ps1, so the whole layer is one language. The rule is kept anyway: it costs
# nothing, and the failure it prevents is one nobody would think to look for when the first .sh
# reappears.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

#   PowerShell handles both endings, so .ps1 files are deliberately left to the repo default
# rather than pinned -- there is no failure to prevent, and pinning them would churn every file
# on the next checkout for no benefit.
